Network Interface Configuration
1. View Current Settings

    nmcli connection show - List all NetworkManager connections.
    
    ip addr show - Display IP addresses for all interfaces.

2. Edit Configuration Files

    nano /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-eth0 - Edit configuration for interface eth0.
    
    Key Parameters:
    
    BOOTPROTO=static - Set to static for a fixed IP or dhcp for dynamic.
    
    IPADDR=192.168.1.100 - The static IP address.
    
    NETMASK=255.255.255.0 - The subnet mask.
    
    GATEWAY=192.168.1.1 - The default gateway.
    
    DNS1=8.8.8.8 - Primary DNS server.
    
    ONBOOT=yes - Enable interface on boot.

3. Restart Networking
    sudo dnf install NetworkManager -y - install network manager

    sudo systemctl enable NetworkManager- enables network manager

    sudo systemctl start NetworkManager-start network manager

    systemctl restart NetworkManager - Apply changes made to config files.

4. Command-Line Tool (nmcli)

    nmcli connection add con-name "MyConnection" ifname eth0 type ethernet ip4 192.168.1.100/24 gw4 192.168.1.1 - Create a new connection,adds a static IP
    
    nmcli connection modify "MyConnection" ipv4.dns "8.8.8.8" - Set DNS for a connection.
    
    nmcli connection up "MyConnection" - Activate a connection.

5. Troubleshooting

    ping google.com - Test network connectivity.
    
    traceroute google.com - Trace the path to a host.


DHCP Server Setup
1. Installation

    sudo dnf install dhclient -y -allows your system to get an IP via DHCP.

    sudo dnf install dhcp-server -y -lets your system serve IPs to other devices.


    sudo dnf install dhcp - Install the DHCP server package.(doesnt work)

2. Configuration File

    nano /etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf - Edit the main configuration file.

3. Static IP Reservation

    Example:
    
    text
    host my-client {
      hardware ethernet 00:66:77:88:99:33;
      fixed-address 192.168.1.10;
    }

4. Service Management

    sudo systemctl enable dhcpd - Enable on boot.
    
    sudo systemctl start dhcpd - Start the service.
    
    sudo systemctl status dhcpd - Check status.

5. Firewall Configuration

    sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=dhcp --permanent - Allow DHCP traffic.
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --reload - Reload firewall rules.

6. Troubleshooting

    cat /var/lib/dhcpd/dhcpd.leases - View active DHCP leases.

DNS Server Setup (BIND)
1. Installation

    sudo dnf install bind - Install the BIND package.

2. Main Configuration

    nano /etc/named.conf - Edit the main config file.
    
    Key Directives:
    
    listen-on port 53 { 192.168.1.10; }; - Specify IP to listen on.
    
    allow-query { 192.168.1.0/24; }; - Define which clients can query.

3. Zone Configuration

    In /etc/named.conf:
    
    text
    zone "example.com" {
      type master;
      file "/var/named/example.com.zone";
    };
4. Zone File Creation

    nano /var/named/example.com.zone - Create a zone file.
    
    Example Records:
    
    text
    @    IN    NS    ns1.example.com.
    @    IN    A     192.168.1.1
    www  IN    A     192.168.1.2
5. Service Management

    sudo systemctl start named - Start the DNS service.
    
    sudo systemctl enable named - Enable on boot.

6. Validation & Testing

    sudo named-checkconf - Check main config for errors.
    
    dig example.com - Test DNS resolution.

7. Firewall Configuration

    sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=dns --permanent - Allow DNS traffic.
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --reload - Reload firewall.


Network Time Synchronization (NTP)
1. Installation

    sudo dnf install ntp - Install the NTP package.

2. Configuration

    nano /etc/ntp.conf - Edit the configuration file.
    
    Specify Time Servers:
    
    text
    server 0.fedora.pool.ntp.org iburst
    server 1.fedora.pool.ntp.org iburst
3. Service Management

    sudo systemctl start ntpd - Start the NTP service.
    
    sudo systemctl enable ntpd - Enable on boot.

4. Verification

    sudo systemctl status ntpd - Check service status.
    
    ntpq -p - View synchronization status with peers.

5. Firewall Configuration

    sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=ntp --permanent - Allow NTP traffic.
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --reload - Reload firewall.

6. Timezone Setting

    timedatectl set-timezone Your/Timezone - Set the server timezone.
    
    timedatectl status - View current time and timezone settings.

